course: everyday
lesson: 453
title: Lied to by someone you love
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: For months Ava's dad told her «everything's fine» while he and Mum were splitting up. There were whispers, closed doors and tight smiles. When they finally told her, Ava said, «I knew. Everyone was whispering.» She was hurt that they hadn't trusted her. Dad said, «You're right, love. We should've told you sooner.» Grown-ups often hide hard things to protect children — but children usually notice that something is wrong, and not knowing can be scarier than the truth.
explain: People we love sometimes lie about worries. Sophie's mum said the school trip money was «no problem»; later Sophie heard her on the phone asking to borrow money, and felt guilty. Mum hugged her: «It's my job to worry about money, not yours. But thank you for caring.» Ben's gran said her hospital visit was «just a check-up». It was something bigger, and Ben found out from his cousin. He told Gran it hurt to hear it that way. Gran held his hand and told him everything, gently. When someone you love keeps a scary truth from you, it's usually out of love — and it's still okay to ask for the truth.
explain: Some lies come from shame. Oisin's da said he'd stopped smoking. Oisin could smell it on his coat. «Da, I can smell it.» His da sat down. «Fair play to you» — in Ireland that means «fair enough, well done». «I'm trying. It's hard.» They made a plan together. Grace's dad told her that her hamster was «sleeping a lot». It had died, and he bought a new one, hoping she wouldn't notice. She noticed — the patch was the wrong colour — and was more upset about the swap than the death. Dad said sorry, and they had a little funeral for the first hamster. Children can handle sad truths, especially when they're told with love.
explain: Not every broken promise is a lie. Kai's mum promised «no more moving houses». A year later they moved again, because of her job and the rent. Kai felt lied to. Mum said, «I didn't lie, love, I hoped. I should've said I hoped.» Zara's best friend said she was moving school «maybe next year» — and went the next month; she couldn't face saying goodbye. Mia's big sister promised the cinema on Saturday, then went with her mates and said «Something came up.» Mia saw her photos and felt crushed; her sister said sorry, and they went the next Saturday.
explain: You can love someone and still not trust their promises. Leo's uncle in Pittsburgh always said he'd «pay you back next week, buddy» for borrowed pocket money — and never did. Leo's mum told him to stop lending; Leo was sad, because he liked his uncle. Both can be true. Jayden's mom answered the water-park question with «We'll see», which in their house meant no. «Mom, just say no.» She laughed. «Okay. No. Maybe in the summer.» It's fine to ask the people you love for clear answers. Most will give them.
fact: Grown-ups sometimes hide hard news, like a family splitting up, to protect children.
fact: Children often notice when something is wrong — whispering, closed doors, tense smiles.
fact: Not knowing can be scarier than knowing; it is okay to say «I know something's going on. Please tell me.»
fact: Grown-ups sometimes hide money worries; worrying about money is their job, not the child's.
fact: Hearing hard news from someone else instead of from the person can hurt; saying so can help.
fact: People sometimes lie about things they are ashamed of, like failing to stop a habit; saying it kindly can help them be honest.
fact: Children can usually handle sad truths, like a pet dying, when they are told with love.
fact: Some broken promises are hopes that did not come true rather than lies.
fact: A «maybe» can hide a «definitely» when someone dreads giving bad news.
fact: You can love someone and still not trust their promises; both can be true.
fact: «We'll see» often means a soft no; it is okay to ask for a clear answer.
fact: People we love most can hurt us most; telling them how it felt is how things get better.
fact: «Da» (Ireland) means dad; «fair play» (Ireland) means fair enough or well done; «mates» means friends; «soz» is slang for sorry.
exercise: What did Ava's dad keep saying while her parents were splitting up? | «Everything's fine» | «We're moving to the seaside» | «Mum's away on holiday» | «Nothing will ever change»
exercise: How did Ava already know something was wrong? | Everyone was whispering | She read a letter on the table | Her teacher told her after class | She saw it on the TV news
exercise: What did Ava's dad say when she said «I knew»? | We should've told you sooner | You were wrong to guess | It's none of your business, you're only a child | Let's not talk about it
exercise: What did Sophie overhear about the trip money? | Mum asking to borrow money on the phone | Mum saying the school trip was cancelled for everyone | Mum winning money in a raffle | Mum telling Dad it was free
exercise: What did Sophie's mum say about worrying about money? | It's my job, not yours, but thank you | You should get a paper round to help with the bills | Never talk about money again | Money is nothing to worry about
exercise: Why was Ben upset about Gran's hospital news? | He heard it from his cousin, not from Gran | Gran was cross with him | His cousin got a present and he didn't get one | He wasn't allowed to visit
exercise: Why did Oisin's da lie about smoking? | He was ashamed he'd started again | He wanted Oisin to try smoking with him too | He thought smoking was healthy | He had never smoked at all
exercise: What did Oisin and his da do after the truth came out? | Made a plan together | Never talked about it again | Hid the cigarettes better | Went to the shop together
exercise: How did Grace know the new hamster was different? | The patch was the wrong colour | It was a different size cage | It could talk a little | It had a name tag
exercise: What upset Grace more than the hamster dying? | Being tricked with a swap | The hamster's funeral | Her dad crying | The new hamster biting her
exercise: What did Kai's mum say about the «no more moving» promise? | I didn't lie, I hoped — I should've said so | I lied on purpose to trick you | I never said that, you're wrong, you must have dreamed it | Moving is fun, stop moaning
exercise: Why did Zara's friend say «maybe next year»? | She couldn't face saying goodbye | She didn't know where she lived | She wanted to stay at the school | She had forgotten about it
exercise: What did Mia's sister say instead of the truth? | «Something came up.» | «I'm ill today, sorry.» | «The cinema is shut.» | «You're too young for it.»
exercise: What did Leo's mum tell him about lending to his uncle? | To stop lending to him | To lend him more money | To ask for double back | To tell the police
exercise: What did «we'll see» mean in Jayden's house? | No | Yes | Next week | Ask Dad
exercise: What can be true at the same time about Leo's uncle? | You can love him and not trust his promises | You must hate him because of the money | You must trust him because you love him, whatever he does | You can't feel anything about him
test: Mum, smiling tightly: «It's nothing, love.» Doors keep closing, whispers. What can you say? | I know something's going on. Please tell me | Okay, I'll just ignore it | I'll find out by listening at doors when you're talking | It's nothing, so I'll forget it
test: Da in Dublin: «Fair play to you, son. I'm trying.» What does «fair play» mean here? | Fair enough, well done | Let's play a game | That's not fair | Play nicely
test: Uncle: «pay ya back next week, buddy, swear». It's the fifth time. What's wise? | Love him, but stop lending | Lend more to be kind | Shout at him in front of everyone | Hide his wallet
test: Mom: «We'll see.» Jayden: «Mom, just say no.» What is Jayden asking for? | A clear answer | A bigger present | Another «maybe» | A longer think about it
test: Kai: «you LIED, you said no more moving!» Mum: «I hoped, love.» What's the difference? | A hope that failed isn't the same as a lie | There is no difference at all, both are broken promises | Hopes are always lies | Lies are always hopes
test: What would happen if Grace's dad had said «I'm so sorry, love, Hammy died»? | Grace would be sad but not tricked | Grace would never find out | Grace would want three hamsters | Grace would be angry for years
test: Big sis: «Something came up, soz.» Her photos show the cinema with her mates. What could Mia say? | That really hurt — you could've just told me | Nothing — sisters always do that | I'm going to lie to you too, to see how you like it | I'll tell Mum you're horrible
test: Kid hears Mum on the phone borrowing money for his trip. He feels awful. What would Mum most likely say? | It's my job to worry, not yours | You should pay me back now | You must not go on the trip | You heard nothing, go to bed
test: In a film, a parent hides a big secret for years and the child never notices. What's more common in real life? | Children notice something is wrong | Children never notice anything | Parents never hide anything from their children | Secrets disappear by themselves
test: Ben hears about Gran's illness from his cousin. What's a brave thing Ben can do? | Tell Gran it hurt to hear it that way | Pretend he doesn't know | Shout at his cousin | Stop visiting Gran
test: Mate: «maybe I'll move school next year, dunno». She moves next month. Why might she have said maybe? | She couldn't face saying goodbye | She wanted to trick Zara for fun | She didn't know her own school | She loves telling lies
exam: Identify the principal reason for which adults frequently conceal grave family news from children. | A wish to protect the children from distress | A wish to deceive the children for amusement | A legal requirement to withhold such news until the children are older | A belief that children never notice anything
exam: It is given that a parent promised that the family would not move again, and that the family subsequently moved owing to employment. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The promise may have been a sincere hope that was not fulfilled | The promise was necessarily a deliberate falsehood | The promise was fulfilled in all respects | Promises concerning residence are invalid
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between affection for a person and trust in that person's promises? | Affection may coexist with a justified distrust of the person's promises | Affection invariably requires complete trust | Distrust invariably destroys affection | Neither affection nor trust can be felt for relatives
exam: Determine which of the following is most commonly reported by children informed of a pet's death with honesty and affection. | That they are able to bear the sad truth | That they suffer more than through deception | That they do not notice the death | That they request a replacement immediately
